
  [1mThe happy path[0m

  1. Page load: [38;5;153midentity.js[39m runs first and defines [38;5;153mIdentity[39m. It reads nothing at load time — expiry is checked on read,
     so there's no startup work and no timer.
  2. Submit → [38;5;153mpreventDefault()[39m → read both inputs → [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m (unchanged).
  3. If valid: [38;5;153mawait login(username, password)[39m.
  4. [38;5;153mlogin[39m stays a stub, but an [3masync[0m one, returning [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username, userId: "stub-" + username }[39m.
     Keeping the existing [38;5;153muser[39m field means nothing that reads today's return value breaks. The [38;5;153mstub-[39m prefix makes it
     obvious in a console that this is not a real server id, and varying it by username is what lets you exercise the
     different-user overwrite by hand.
  5. On [38;5;153msuccess[39m: [38;5;153mIdentity.set(result.userId)[39m. Then the existing result log.
  6. Later, any other form calls [38;5;153mIdentity.get()[39m and gets the id or [38;5;153mnull[39m.

  [1mError handling[0m — the governing rule: [3midentity tracking must never break logging in.[0m It's a secondary concern riding on
  a primary flow, so every failure in it degrades to "no id" rather than to an exception.

  - [1m[38;5;153mlogin[39m returns [38;5;153msuccess: false[0m — store nothing, and leave any existing record untouched. A failed attempt is not
    evidence about who is at the keyboard, so it shouldn't clear a valid id.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlogin[39m throws[0m (what a real network call will do) — the handler wraps the [38;5;153mawait[39m in [38;5;153mtry[39m/[38;5;153mcatch[39m, logs the error, stores
    nothing. Today's stub can't throw; the handling goes in now because the real one will.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m throws or is absent[0m — disabled cookies/storage, private-browsing quota, sandboxed iframes. [38;5;153mset[39m catches
    and warns once; [38;5;153mget[39m catches and returns [38;5;153mnull[39m. Login still completes.
  - [1mMalformed record[0m — unparseable JSON, missing [38;5;153muserId[39m, or an unrecognized [38;5;153mv[39m. Treated as absent and removed on read.
  - [1m[38;5;153mstoredAt[39m in the future[0m — clock changes make this real, and a future timestamp would otherwise produce a record that
    never expires. Treated as invalid and removed.
  - [1m[38;5;153mset(null)[39m or [38;5;153mset("")[0m — ignored with a warning rather than throwing, for the same reason as above: a bad id is a
    tracking bug, not a reason to fail a login.

  The through-line is that [38;5;153mget()[39m has exactly two outcomes for any caller — a usable id, or [38;5;153mnull[39m. Future forms never have
  to handle a third case.

  Does Section 2 look right? Testing is the last section, then I'll write the spec.

